WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.225
Picture yourself at a café with a laptop.

2
00:00:05.225 --> 00:00:11.567
You type the address of a web page, press Enter, and the page appears in about a twentieth of a second.

3
00:00:11.833 --> 00:00:19.989
It's easy to picture one long wire from your laptop to the computer that sent it, with your request rushing along it in one piece.

4
00:00:20.233 --> 00:00:24.626
But that computer is many links away, behind machines owned by strangers.

5
00:00:24.626 --> 00:00:28.537
So how does a stranger's machine know where to pass your request?

6
00:00:28.537 --> 00:00:33.049
And when it arrives, how does the far computer know which program it's for?

7
00:00:33.049 --> 00:00:34.553
Hold those two questions.

8
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.695
Let's look at the request.

9
00:00:36.695 --> 00:00:49.085
Ask curl, a program that fetches web pages from the command line, for example.com, and it writes these 74 bytes of HTTP, the language that browsers and web servers speak.

10
00:00:49.333 --> 00:00:50.886
Now notice what's missing.

11
00:00:50.886 --> 00:00:58.115
There's a site name, but no number for the computer, no number for the program, and nothing the card next door could use.

12
00:00:58.333 --> 00:01:02.690
curl hands these bytes to the kernel, the core of the operating system.

13
00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:05.941
On the way down, the kernel has to add every address.

14
00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:12.073
What we want: every machine on the way should find the one address it needs, without reading your letter.

15
00:01:12.333 --> 00:01:13.981
Think of posting a letter.

16
00:01:13.981 --> 00:01:15.312
You write the letter.

17
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.551
It goes in an envelope with a flat number on it.

18
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.369
That envelope goes inside a parcel with the street address.

19
00:01:22.633 --> 00:01:25.421
Then a courier sticks a label on the outside.

20
00:01:25.421 --> 00:01:28.023
It says only which depot to drive to next.

21
00:01:28.267 --> 00:01:30.727
Each wrapper treats what's inside as sealed.

22
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.939
Now the same picture in your laptop.

23
00:01:33.939 --> 00:01:37.776
The letter is curl's request: 74 bytes of HTTP.

24
00:01:38.033 --> 00:01:42.157
Each layer puts a header in front: a few bytes with its own addresses.

25
00:01:42.433 --> 00:01:49.987
The envelope is TCP's header, from the layer that picks the program and keeps the bytes in order.

26
00:01:49.987 --> 00:01:52.245
32 bytes, with the two ports.

27
00:01:52.245 --> 00:01:53.803
74 bytes become 106.

28
00:01:54.033 --> 00:02:02.878
The parcel is IP's header: 20 bytes, with the two computers' IP addresses and a counter called the time to live.

29
00:02:02.878 --> 00:02:03.668
126 bytes.

30
00:02:03.867 --> 00:02:12.748
The courier label is Ethernet's header: 14 bytes, with two MAC addresses, the names of network cards on this one link.

31
00:02:12.748 --> 00:02:14.931
140 bytes, sent as one frame.

32
00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.530
This wrapping is called encapsulation.

33
00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:21.208
Each layer wraps what it was handed, and never looks inside.

34
00:02:21.467 --> 00:02:25.022
That's 66 bytes of wrapping around 74 bytes of letter.

35
00:02:25.022 --> 00:02:26.865
Online shops would be proud.

36
00:02:26.865 --> 00:02:30.617
But every wrapper earns its place, as we're about to see.

37
00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:32.378
Back to the long wire.

38
00:02:32.378 --> 00:02:33.586
It isn't one wire.

39
00:02:33.586 --> 00:02:40.840
Your laptop's card can only reach the cards on its own link, so the label names the next card: the router's.

40
00:02:41.067 --> 00:02:47.111
A router is a machine on two links that passes packets from one to the next, like a courier's depot.

41
00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:52.090
When the frame leaves the depot for the next link, which labels will have changed?

42
00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:53.012
Pause and guess.

43
00:02:56.233 --> 00:03:03.664
The router takes off the courier label, reads the street address, and lowers the time to live from 64 to 63.

44
00:03:03.867 --> 00:03:07.521
Then it sticks on a new label, for the next card on the next link.

45
00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:09.866
It never opened the envelope.

46
00:03:09.866 --> 00:03:13.499
The IP addresses and the ports arrive as they left.

47
00:03:13.499 --> 00:03:15.137
Only the label changed.

48
00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:17.416
So there never was one long wire.

49
00:03:17.416 --> 00:03:21.753
There's a chain of links, and the outer label is rewritten on each one.

50
00:03:21.753 --> 00:03:27.068
That answers our first question: each machine on the way reads only the street address.

51
00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:32.394
A router is the perfect courier: quick, and not the least bit curious.

52
00:03:32.394 --> 00:03:37.852
It never reads TCP or HTTP, so those layers can change without it noticing.

53
00:03:38.067 --> 00:03:39.908
The frame reaches the server.

54
00:03:39.908 --> 00:03:43.273
Its card keeps it, because the label names that card.

55
00:03:43.273 --> 00:03:46.193
Now the kernel unwraps, in the opposite order.

56
00:03:46.467 --> 00:03:49.062
How does it know what's inside each wrapper?

57
00:03:49.062 --> 00:03:51.598
Here our post office picture needs a patch.

58
00:03:51.598 --> 00:03:54.075
A paper envelope doesn't say what's in it.

59
00:03:54.075 --> 00:03:55.432
Every header here does.

60
00:03:55.700 --> 00:04:00.961
Ethernet's type field says 0x0800: an IP packet is inside.

61
00:04:00.961 --> 00:04:02.775
Off comes the label.

62
00:04:03.033 --> 00:04:07.617
IP's protocol field says 6: a TCP segment is inside.

63
00:04:07.617 --> 00:04:09.468
Off comes the parcel.

64
00:04:09.733 --> 00:04:13.783
And TCP's port, 80, picks the program: the web server.

65
00:04:13.783 --> 00:04:16.857
It reads exactly the 74 bytes curl wrote.

66
00:04:16.857 --> 00:04:19.332
That answers our second question.

67
00:04:19.567 --> 00:04:21.717
Every header says what's inside it.

68
00:04:21.717 --> 00:04:24.849
The receiver follows the labels, and never guesses.

69
00:04:25.433 --> 00:04:26.178
Two limits.

70
00:04:26.178 --> 00:04:30.579
Some boxes on the way do rewrite the street address and the port.

71
00:04:30.579 --> 00:04:32.813
Lesson 3 of this track meets one.

72
00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:35.638
And a big answer doesn't fit in one frame.

73
00:04:35.638 --> 00:04:37.572
That comes later in this lesson.

74
00:04:37.867 --> 00:04:39.166
So, one long wire?

75
00:04:39.166 --> 00:04:39.382
No.

76
00:04:39.382 --> 00:04:45.588
A letter in three wrappers, and a chain of depots that each read only the outside one.

77
00:04:45.833 --> 00:04:48.453
On the way down, each layer adds a header.

78
00:04:48.453 --> 00:04:51.820
On the way up, each layer reads one, and takes it off.

79
00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:53.113
One quick check.

80
00:04:53.113 --> 00:04:55.140
Put a second router on the path.

81
00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:58.751
When the frame leaves it, what will the time to live say?

82
00:04:58.751 --> 00:04:59.764
Pause and think.

83
00:05:02.933 --> 00:05:03.154
62.

84
00:05:03.154 --> 00:05:07.118
Each router lowers it by one, and puts on a new label.

85
00:05:07.118 --> 00:05:10.569
Everything inside the label arrives as it left.
